  "worked_solution": "First, we reduce our congruence to a congruence of the form $ax≡b,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-25 & ≡20\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,21) \\\\ 4x-25+25 & ≡20+25\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,21) \\\\ 4x & ≡45\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,21) \\\\ 4x & ≡2\\cdot 21+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,21) \\\\ 4x & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,21)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=4$ and $n=21$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(4,21)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,21).$ To find the inverse of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,21)$, we compute the exponents of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,21)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,21):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2} & ≡16 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,21) \\\\ {4}^{3} & ≡16\\cdot 4≡64≡3\\cdot 21+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,21)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $16\\cdot 4≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,21),$ which means that $16$ is the multiplicative inverse of $4$ (modulo $21$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $16,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,21) \\\\ 16\\cdot 4x & ≡16\\cdot 3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,21) \\\\ (16\\cdot 4)x & ≡48 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,21) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡6+2\\cdot 21 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,21) \\\\ x & ≡6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,21)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,21).$",
